b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲
  c̲o̲m̲m̲e̲n̲t̲ HARNESS POSING reposed;
  c̲o̲m̲m̲e̲n̲t̲ PROVENANCE author: implementation-algol-60, an agent (Claude Opus 5.5, claude-opus-5-5);
  c̲o̲m̲m̲e̲n̲t̲ PROVENANCE precursor seen: no;
  c̲o̲m̲m̲e̲n̲t̲ PROVENANCE idioms: the Revised Report on ALGOL 60 (1962) for blocks and arrays whose bounds are computed at run time (5.2.4.2), recursive procedures with parameters called by name (4.7.3.2, 5.4) and procedures as actual parameters (4.7.5, 5.4.4), each solution being passed to a parameterless procedure declared in an inner block, in the way of the procedure B passed as a parameter in Knuth's man or boy test (1964, examples/man-or-boy-1964.alg in the image), the for statement (4.6) and conditional statements and expressions (3.3, 4.5), the X1 system's input and output procedures read, print, PRINTTEXT and NLCR, as the harness README gives them (harnesses/algol-60/README.md, 2.1) and its own X1 programs cite them (Kruseman Aretz 2003, chapter 7), and the X1 compiler's representation, with underlined reserved words and the operator symbols (harness README, 2.1), sequences as parallel arrays of heads and tail indices (systems file, Values), and the observe block of the harness README example X01 (harnesses/algol-60/README.md, 2.8);
  c̲o̲m̲m̲e̲n̲t̲ CONSTANTS a line 1: L e1 ... eL, the length and then the elements of the known sequence of the query alist(s). An atom is its integer code: the atom a of the definition is 1, any other atom another integer;
  c̲o̲m̲m̲e̲n̲t̲ P04c. The query alist(s) with s known, and the definition alist-left. Each relation is a procedure that passes each of its solutions to the procedure found, and returns when it has no more. A sequence is a cell index into the arrays hd and tl, 0 being the empty sequence, and a cell c being the sequence hd[c] followed by the sequence tl[c]. An unknown sequence is a Boolean that says whether it has a value and an integer that holds it. The cases are tried in the order written, and the conjuncts of case 1 in the order written, so that alist(m) is called with m unknown before s = a ++ m is decided. A value given to an unknown is taken back when found returns, and a cell made for it is given back;
  i̲n̲t̲e̲g̲e̲r̲ n, cap, m, i, code, k, len;
  c̲o̲m̲m̲e̲n̲t̲ harness words: the instance line, and the one message, which poses stage 1;
  n := read; cap := read; m := read;
  f̲o̲r̲ i := 1 s̲t̲e̲p̲ 1 u̲n̲t̲i̲l̲ m d̲o̲ code := read;
  code := read; k := read;
  c̲o̲m̲m̲e̲n̲t̲ the stage's constants: the length of s, which sizes the cells, and then its elements;
  len := read;
  b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲
  i̲n̲t̲e̲g̲e̲r̲ top, acode, sv, al1, al2;
  b̲o̲o̲l̲e̲a̲n̲ sk;
  i̲n̲t̲e̲g̲e̲r̲ a̲r̲r̲a̲y̲ hd, tl[0:len + 20000];

  i̲n̲t̲e̲g̲e̲r̲ p̲r̲o̲c̲e̲d̲u̲r̲e̲ cons(h, t);
    v̲a̲l̲u̲e̲ h, t; i̲n̲t̲e̲g̲e̲r̲ h, t;
  c̲o̲m̲m̲e̲n̲t̲ a new cell, the sequence h followed by the sequence t;
  b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲ top := top + 1; hd[top] := h; tl[top] := t; cons := top e̲n̲d̲ cons;

  i̲n̲t̲e̲g̲e̲r̲ p̲r̲o̲c̲e̲d̲u̲r̲e̲ readseq(len);
    v̲a̲l̲u̲e̲ len; i̲n̲t̲e̲g̲e̲r̲ len;
  c̲o̲m̲m̲e̲n̲t̲ a sequence of len elements, read from the input in order;
  b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲
    i̲n̲t̲e̲g̲e̲r̲ j, e, first, last, c;
    first := last := 0;
    f̲o̲r̲ j := 1 s̲t̲e̲p̲ 1 u̲n̲t̲i̲l̲ len d̲o̲
    b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲
      e := read; c := cons(e, 0);
      i̲f̲ last = 0 t̲h̲e̲n̲ first := c e̲l̲s̲e̲ tl[last] := c;
      last := c
    e̲n̲d̲;
    readseq := first
  e̲n̲d̲ readseq;

  b̲o̲o̲l̲e̲a̲n̲ p̲r̲o̲c̲e̲d̲u̲r̲e̲ same(p, q);
    v̲a̲l̲u̲e̲ p, q; i̲n̲t̲e̲g̲e̲r̲ p, q;
  c̲o̲m̲m̲e̲n̲t̲ the equality of two sequences;
  same := i̲f̲ p = 0 t̲h̲e̲n̲ q = 0 e̲l̲s̲e̲ i̲f̲ q = 0 t̲h̲e̲n̲ f̲a̲l̲s̲e̲ e̲l̲s̲e̲ i̲f̲ hd[p] = hd[q] t̲h̲e̲n̲ same(tl[p], tl[q]) e̲l̲s̲e̲ f̲a̲l̲s̲e̲;

  p̲r̲o̲c̲e̲d̲u̲r̲e̲ conseq(sk, s, mk, m, found);
    b̲o̲o̲l̲e̲a̲n̲ sk, mk; i̲n̲t̲e̲g̲e̲r̲ s, m; p̲r̲o̲c̲e̲d̲u̲r̲e̲ found;
  c̲o̲m̲m̲e̲n̲t̲ the equation s = the sequence of a alone ++ m, by the places known. With s known, it gives m its value, or tests it. With s unknown and m known, it gives s its value. Each solution is passed to found. With both unknown it does nothing: that is not reached here, since case 1 decides it only after alist(m) has given m;
  i̲f̲ sk t̲h̲e̲n̲
  b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲
    i̲f̲ s ≠ 0 t̲h̲e̲n̲
    b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲
      i̲f̲ hd[s] = acode t̲h̲e̲n̲
      b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲
        i̲f̲ mk t̲h̲e̲n̲ b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲ i̲f̲ same(tl[s], m) t̲h̲e̲n̲ found e̲n̲d̲
        e̲l̲s̲e̲ b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲ m := tl[s]; mk := t̲r̲u̲e̲; found; mk := f̲a̲l̲s̲e̲ e̲n̲d̲
      e̲n̲d̲
    e̲n̲d̲
  e̲n̲d̲
  e̲l̲s̲e̲ i̲f̲ mk t̲h̲e̲n̲
  b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲ s := cons(acode, m); sk := t̲r̲u̲e̲; found; sk := f̲a̲l̲s̲e̲; top := top - 1 e̲n̲d̲ conseq;

  p̲r̲o̲c̲e̲d̲u̲r̲e̲ alist(sk, s, found);
    b̲o̲o̲l̲e̲a̲n̲ sk; i̲n̲t̲e̲g̲e̲r̲ s; p̲r̲o̲c̲e̲d̲u̲r̲e̲ found;
  c̲o̲m̲m̲e̲n̲t̲ alist-left, its cases in order. Case 1: alist(s) if alist(m) and s = the sequence of a alone ++ m, with the two conjuncts in that order. Case 2: alist(the empty sequence);
  b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲
    b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲
      i̲n̲t̲e̲g̲e̲r̲ m;
      b̲o̲o̲l̲e̲a̲n̲ mk;
      p̲r̲o̲c̲e̲d̲u̲r̲e̲ second;
      c̲o̲m̲m̲e̲n̲t̲ the second conjunct of case 1, once alist(m) has given m;
      conseq(sk, s, mk, m, found);
      al1 := al1 + 1;
      mk := f̲a̲l̲s̲e̲;
      alist(mk, m, second)
    e̲n̲d̲;
    i̲f̲ sk t̲h̲e̲n̲
    b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲ i̲f̲ s = 0 t̲h̲e̲n̲ b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲ al2 := al2 + 1; found e̲n̲d̲ e̲n̲d̲
    e̲l̲s̲e̲
    b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲ al2 := al2 + 1; s := 0; sk := t̲r̲u̲e̲; found; sk := f̲a̲l̲s̲e̲ e̲n̲d̲
  e̲n̲d̲ alist;

  p̲r̲o̲c̲e̲d̲u̲r̲e̲ answer;
  c̲o̲m̲m̲e̲n̲t̲ output adapter: one solution. The query has no unknowns, so a solution is true;
  b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲ PRINTTEXT(`[answer]true[/answer]'); NLCR e̲n̲d̲;

  top := al1 := al2 := 0;
  acode := 1;
  sv := readseq(len); sk := t̲r̲u̲e̲;

  c̲o̲m̲m̲e̲n̲t̲ the query: alist(s), with s known;
  alist(sk, sv, answer);

  c̲o̲m̲m̲e̲n̲t̲ observe: the applications of each case of alist;
  PRINTTEXT(`[observe]');
  PRINTTEXT(`[operations alist/1 '); print(al1); b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲ PRINTTEXT(`]'); NLCR e̲n̲d̲;
  PRINTTEXT(`[operations alist/2 '); print(al2); b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲ PRINTTEXT(`]'); NLCR e̲n̲d̲;
  b̲e̲g̲i̲n̲ PRINTTEXT(`[/observe]'); NLCR; PRINTTEXT(`[end]') e̲n̲d̲
  e̲n̲d̲
e̲n̲d̲
